WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.730 --> 00:00:04.960
Remember when you learned about for loops, if statements, functions?

00:00:04.960 --> 00:00:07.810
Those are fundamental concepts in programming, and if you're

00:00:07.810 --> 00:00:11.430
ever writing code in other languages, you'll see these concepts again and again.

00:00:11.430 --> 00:00:15.980
Now, the same goes for certain concepts with code organizations.

00:00:15.980 --> 00:00:20.280
Here are some of those main concepts that you'll see over and over again.

00:00:20.280 --> 00:00:24.950
The first on our list of fundamental organizational concepts is models.

00:00:24.950 --> 00:00:27.140
Now, we've already talked at these.

00:00:27.140 --> 00:00:29.030
Models just represent your data,

00:00:29.030 --> 00:00:33.680
and models can be stupid, like a JavaScript object literal as shown here.

00:00:33.680 --> 00:00:38.320
Or they can be more intelligent, as in most other organizational frameworks, and

00:00:38.320 --> 00:00:40.280
as we'll see in a bit.

00:00:40.280 --> 00:00:42.060
Next, we have collections.

00:00:42.060 --> 00:00:44.110
Collections are basically smart arrays.

00:00:45.110 --> 00:00:46.740
And what are they filled with?

00:00:46.740 --> 00:00:47.750
Models.

00:00:47.750 --> 00:00:49.740
Now, remember, models are just data.

00:00:49.740 --> 00:00:53.710
Even if they're intelligent models, they still represent data.

00:00:53.710 --> 00:00:57.070
So, collections of models are collections of data,

00:00:57.070 --> 00:01:01.210
just like our cats array in the previous lesson, but a little bit smarter.

00:01:01.210 --> 00:01:04.629
Now, we've talked about octopuses at length, but if you bring them up

00:01:04.629 --> 00:01:08.230
to any other developer, they won't know what you're talking about.

00:01:08.230 --> 00:01:13.070
Octopuses in the real world are called things like controllers or view models.

00:01:14.340 --> 00:01:17.770
Here's another thing we've already talked about in-depth before.

00:01:17.770 --> 00:01:20.360
Views are the things that draw the interface and

00:01:20.360 --> 00:01:22.700
allow the user to interact with the interface.

00:01:24.010 --> 00:01:26.410
We also have a similar thing called routers.

00:01:27.530 --> 00:01:31.420
Now, this is new to you technically, but only kind of.

00:01:31.420 --> 00:01:37.180
Routers keep track of the state of the URL, which in a way is a view-like thing.

00:01:37.180 --> 00:01:40.990
The URL is something that the user can interact with and see.

00:01:40.990 --> 00:01:44.540
Often, routers are used to track the state of the application.

00:01:44.540 --> 00:01:47.960
Now, we're not going to get into routers in any more depth than this,

00:01:47.960 --> 00:01:49.040
just conceptually.

00:01:49.040 --> 00:01:54.160
But just know that they're very similar to views, just for the URL bar up here.

00:01:54.160 --> 00:01:55.070
Don't be afraid of them.

00:01:55.070 --> 00:01:59.110
If you can understand views, you can understand routers.

00:01:59.110 --> 00:02:03.210
Now, organizational libraries often look very different from each other.

00:02:03.210 --> 00:02:05.280
But no matter which you use,

00:02:05.280 --> 00:02:09.820
you generally see these same five things over and over and over again.

00:02:09.820 --> 00:02:12.500
The concepts are all the same, and

00:02:12.500 --> 00:02:16.570
that means once we have a solid understanding of these basic concepts,

00:02:16.570 --> 00:02:19.920
we can feel empowered to learn new organizational libraries.

